<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Organisation of African Unity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Organisation of African Unity was an intergovernmental body of independent African states founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> in 1963. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> took a leading part in its creation. Its establishment confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s role as a diplomatic centre of African independence, an important legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, and it was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> in 2002.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity.jpg" alt="Organisation of African Unity" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Organization of African Unity · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Organization_of_African_Unity_(1970%E2%80%932002);_Flag_of_the_African_Union_(2004%E2%80%932010).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The OAU began with the Sanniquellie Pledge at the First West African Summit Conference, held at Sanniquellie, Liberia, from 15 to 19 July 1959. President Tubman of Liberia hosted President Touré of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and Prime Minister Nkrumah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and the three pledged to work for a Community of Independent African States. After independence, two groups disagreed on how unity should be reached. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> bloc, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> and founded in 1961, wanted a federation of all African countries. The Monrovian bloc, led by Senghor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, preferred gradual unity through economic cooperation. The dispute was resolved when Emperor Haile Selassie I invited both groups to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, where the organisation was established on 25 May 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The OAU's primary aims were to coordinate and intensify cooperation among African states for a better life, to defend their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and territorial integrity, and to eradicate colonialism and white minority rule. A Liberation Committee aided independence movements. The OAU gave weapons, training and bases to groups such as the ANC and PAC against apartheid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> against the government of Rhodesia, closed African harbours and airspace to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> to expel South Africa from bodies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a>. It also helped to set up the African Development Bank and worked with the UN on refugees.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> left on 12 November 1984, after the admission in 1982 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a> as the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>. By the time of its dissolution 53 of the 54 African states were members. In September 1999 the OAU issued the Sirte Declaration, calling for a new body, and on 9 July 2002 its chairman, the South African president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thabo-mbeki">Thabo Mbeki</a>, formally dissolved it and replaced it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The organisation had no armed force of its own and held to a policy of non-interference in the affairs of member states, so it could do little to stop civil wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> or abuses such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>'s Uganda in the 1970s. Critics called it a talking shop and, because it did little to protect citizens from their own leaders, a Dictators' Club. Divisions between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> camps, led by Nkrumah on the socialist side and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> on the capitalist side, made agreement on action rare. Kofi Annan praised it for bringing Africans together, and its members often acted together in the UN to defend African interests, especially against lingering colonialism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Organisation of African Unity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The founding of the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b> in Addis Ababa in 1963, under Haile Selassie's patronage, gave this role an institution and made Ethiopia the diplomatic capital of independent Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It belongs to the Arab League, the Arab Maghreb Union and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation; it left the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b> in 1984 when the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic was admitted and rejoined the African Union in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…refuge for formerly enslaved people from the Americas, it became, with Ethiopia, one of only two African states to remain independent during the European partition of the continent, and it was a founding member of the League of Nations, the United Nations and the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nyerere was one of the most respected leaders of independent Africa, a founder of the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b> in 1963 and a prominent voice of the Non-Aligned Movement, and his writings on ujamaa and self-reliance influenced development thinking far beyond Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The compromise was the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b>, whose charter thirty-two states signed in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963 at a summit hosted by Emperor Haile Selassie.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial borders became national frontiers, which the <b>Organisation of African Unity</b> resolved in 1964 to respect.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
